  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers this afternoon and tonight. \nBrief gusty winds or a rumble of thunder possible. Mainly dry \nand cooler Saturday. \n\nDay Cloud Phase satellite imagery and radar this afternoon show\nshower development increasing in coverage across portions of\nsouthern New England. This activity will continue to expand and\npush eastward into the early evening along and ahead of a\ngradually advancing cold front. \n\nRecent SPC mesoanalysis highlights an environment conducive to\nconvective elements with PWATs near 1.6\", marginal instability,\nand steep low level lapse rates. The well mixed boundary layer\nhas contributed to an axis of elevated DCAPE values around 1000\nJ/kg extending from east CT to a portion of east MA. Given this\nset-up, expect scattered convective showers this evening. A few\nshowers may be capable of a rumble of thunder, brief downpour,\nand localized gusty winds. Although not expecting severe\ncriteria. \n\nThe front and associated showers will slowly push through\ntonight. Showers exit most areas by midnight, with the exception\nof southeast MA and the Cape/Islands where showers may linger\nthrough around 5 AM. Skies clear behind the departing showers\nSaturday morning with post-frontal winds out of the northwest.\nColder air advects in post-front for Saturday with 850 mb\ntemperatures +4-6C. This will yield a more fall-like day with\ntemperatures in the mid to upper 60s for most of the southern\nNew England. As surface high pressure shifts east, flow weakens\nand shift to the east across the eastern coastal areas. Onshore\nmay keep temperatures in those areas locked in the low to mid\n60s. \n\nRadiational cooling should optimize cooling Saturday night given\nthe clear skies with lows dropping in the 40s for the interior\nand low 50s near the coast. The typical radiator spots and\nhigher terrain could drop into the upper 30s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front brings a chance for scattered \nshowers Sunday followed by much cooler temperatures for the first \nweek of October. \n\nShower chances Sunday have come into better focus with the latest \nguidance showing a band of anomalously high PWATs advecting poleward \nalong and ahead of a strong cold front Sunday night into Monday \nmorning. While most guidance has shifted towards more widespread \nshowers the fly in the ointment for this forecast is the depth and \nextent of a layer of dry air (RH <35%). More widespread dry air \naloft may serve to help keep the showers more scattered in nature as \ninstead of a broad shield of rainfall that some global guidance is \ndepicting. \n\nWill see strong CAA behind the front later Monday into Tuesday as \nthe parent low deepens over the Canadian Maritimes. Ensemble \nguidance shows a 30-50% chance of the low deepening >980mb which is \nan increase from previous runs. A deeper low would bring stronger CAA \nand thus colder 850 mb temps. At present, guidance shows the 0C \nisotherm making it as far south as coastal MA and CT by Tuesday \nmorning. These cold mid-level temperatures likely translate to highs \nin the mid and lower 50s Tuesday and Wednesday. More notably, low \ntemps likely fall into the 30s Monday and Tuesday night. Monday \nnight should see the <40 degree readings limited to northern MA and \nthe higher elevations of eastern CT as the boundary layer stays \nbetter mixed. More widespread 30-40 degree readings arrive Tuesday \nnight as the center of continental high pressure crests over the \nregion resulting in calm winds and clear skies. Thus, the best \nchance of the season's first widespread frost/freeze should arrive \nTuesday night. \n\nThe high then pushes east by Thursday, marking the return of \npredominantly southwest flow and seasonable temperature to southern \nNew England for the end of the week.",
